description.narrative:
2.4. Filing of a Public Interest Litigation (PIL) Case

Iten ELC Petition No. 007 of 2022 – Legal Advice Centre T/A Kituo cha Sheria & Anor v. Attorney General and 7 Others
KITUO filed a public interest case on behalf of members of marginalized and minority groups (and their ranks include extremely vulnerable groups such as children, women, youth, persons with disabilities and the elderly) living around Lake Baringo. The petition was provoked by several egregious constitutional violations directly linked to climate change impacts and rising water levels in Lake Baringo. Lake Baringo basin is mainly inhabited by the Ilchamus; Pokot and Tugen communities, who have lived on the lake’s shores as their ancestral home and eked out a livelihood from socio-economic activities enabled by the lake such as pastoralism, agriculture, fishing, and tourism among others. The region around Lake Baringo is home to approximately 5,000 households and is presently occupied by several hotels, and recreation facilities. It also hosts rural infrastructure, agricultural fields and grazing areas, churches, dispensaries, health centres, schools, and other social amenities.

In November 2019, the water levels in Lake Baringo began rising steadily with increased rainfall and increased inflows from perennial rivers that drain into the lake such as Perkerra and Molo rivers. By 2020, the water levels in Lake Baringo continued rising precipitously to unprecedented levels and affected the Petitioners by submerging roads, agricultural fields and grazing areas, churches, dispensaries, health centres, schools, and electricity lines, and completely destroyed their businesses and means of livelihood. This affected their lives in these ways: 
i.	Increased infections of malaria due to large swathes of breeding grounds for mosquitoes created by the water; 
ii.	Outbreak of cholera and typhoid due to seepage from submerged toilets that have contaminated drinking water;
iii.	Destruction of crops due to high water table;
iv.	Massive population displacement and loss of land and other forms of property;
v.	Relocation of the Petitioners to Internally Displaced Persons (IDP) camps;
vi.	Increased human-wildlife conflicts due to shrinking habitats and movement of wildlife species closer to human settlements;
vii.	Lost livelihoods, infrastructure and investments that the Petitioners had acquired over years thereby rendering them poor and destitute;
viii.	Loss of employment and income opportunities due to businesses that have been completely destroyed;
ix.	A life of hardship, penury and abject poverty;
x.	Acute deprivation of the constitutional right to a clean and healthy environment; 
xi.	Loss of human dignity.
The community members continue to face acute climate vulnerability. 

A report commissioned by the Ministry of Environment and Forestry in 2020 to study affected water bodies including Lake Baringo stated the cause of rising water levels as being hydro-meteorological variables due to climate change that have led to increased moisture availability as seen in the rainfall data and discharge of the rivers feeding the lakes. There is also increased soil in run off occasioned by land use changes which have increasingly added to the siltation of the lakes as seen in the sediment load in the rivers. All the Rift Valley lakes are situated in faulted terrain in the geologically active Eastern Africa Rift Valley and are therefore mainly controlled by the geological structures. For this reason, the increasing water levels are changing the composition of lake water thus affecting biodiversity. The main reason for the rising water levels is climate change. Evidence for this is provided by the level of rainfall in the catchment areas as documented in the various rainfall gauging stations.

KITUO argued that the State had failed to mainstream climate change responses as articulated in the Climate Change Act No. 11 of 2016. This is coupled with the refusal, failure or neglect to address the basic needs of the Petitioners who are members of marginalized communities and who have been rendered extremely vulnerable by the effects of climate change. The right to equal protection and benefit of the law, human dignity, the right to acquire and own property in any part of Kenya, the right to a clean and healthy environment that is protected for the benefit of present and future generations, rights to access land and be relocated in the event of displacement by natural causes, the right to health, adequate housing, clean and safe water in adequate quantities have been violated.

The Petition places the obligation to ensure comprehensive climate change programmes on the State. It highlights that the State, through its failure to protect against climate change has violated several human rights guaranteed in the Constitution of Kenya. It obligates the State to provide compensation to affected communities, provide shelter and livelihood, provide access to land to squatters and or persons displaced by natural causes; rehabilitate, relocate and restore infrastructure damaged by floods including roads, schools, dispensaries and health centres, electricity supply, water supply, and other essential infrastructure in Lake Baringo; undertake a thorough nation-wide assessment on how climate change elements such as temperature rise, change in precipitation, extreme weather events, sea level rise and seasonal shifts affect floods, drought water shortages (supply and quality), air quality, human health, and habitat loss; to conduct climate change scenarios and develop corresponding policy responses on how these changes will affect human population, infrastructure, the environment, the economy and the Kenyan society as a whole.

Nrb HCCC Constitutional Petition No. E123 of 2021 Kituo cha Sheria and Another –vs- The Attorney-General and 3 Others
The High Court of Kenya sitting in Nairobi temporarily stopped government from closure of camps and repatriating of refugees and asylum seekers living in Kakuma and Dadab camps. The two camps are home to more than 400,000 refugees and asylum seekers from Somalia, South Sudan, Ethiopia, Congo, Uganda among other nationalities. In the petition, Kituo cha Sheria argues that the proposed closure of the camps and repatriation of refugees to their home countries violates the principle of non-refoulement, right to asylum, human dignity, freedom and security of persons, Kenya’s international obligations under the 1951 UN Convention and the OAU Convention. The Petition is also supported by Deborah Thomas Kashindi, a Congolese refugee living in Kakuma refugee camp, who sought asylum in Kenya in February 2014. 

The Honorable Judge gave the following orders:
1.	A conservatory order suspending the 1st and 2nd Respondents from repatriating refugees and asylum seekers dwelling in the camps of Kakuma and Dadaab
2.	That the orders to be upon the Respondents and Interested party within 5 days 
3.	That each party should lodge their response within 14 days 
4.	That a further 14-day period is accorded filing of   supplementary affidavits if need be 
5.	The matters filed were consolidated.  
In the course of the reporting period, the State responded to the Petition indicating that the country was in the position of transitioning into settlements as provided under the Refugee Act 2021. The enactment of the aforesaid Act gave rise to new issues that would require the court’s interpretation. The parties have since argued the case, filed their submissions and await judgment. The Court will issue judgment on notice.
